**Real case: Mining Your Ps and Qs, 2012.** Nadia Heninger, Zakir Durumeric, Eric Wustrow and J. Alex Halderman (UC San Diego and University of Michigan) scanned the whole IPv4 internet for TLS and SSH keys and published the results at [USENIX Security 2012](https://www.usenix.org/conference/usenixsecurity12/technical-sessions/presentation/heninger), where it won best paper. From 5.8 million unique TLS certificates and 6.2 million SSH host keys they found:

- 0.75% of TLS certificates shared keys because of insufficient entropy at key generation;
- RSA private keys computable for 0.50% of TLS hosts and 0.03% of SSH hosts, because different RSA moduli shared a prime factor, which the greatest common divisor reveals;
- DSA (Digital Signature Algorithm) private keys for 1.03% of SSH hosts, from weak signature randomness (the k of lesson 7).

Nearly all were headless or embedded devices (routers, firewalls, server management cards) that generate keys automatically on first boot. They notified 54 manufacturers, and found a boot-time entropy hole in the Linux random number generator.

